  "full_response": "A **Kubernetes (K8s) cluster** is a set of nodes (physical or virtual machines) that run containerized applications managed by Kubernetes. The architecture follows a **master-worker model**, where the **control plane (master)** manages the cluster and **worker nodes** run the application workloads.\n\nHere’s a breakdown of the key components and their roles:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Control Plane (Master Node Components)**\nThe control plane manages the cluster, makes global decisions (e.g., scheduling), and responds to cluster events.\n\n#### **A. API Server (`kube-apiserver`)**\n- **Role**: The **front-end** for the Kubernetes control plane.\n- **Responsibilities**:\n  - Exposes the Kubernetes API (RESTful interface).\n  - Validates and processes requests (e.g., `kubectl` commands).\n  - Acts as the gateway for all cluster interactions.\n  - Ensures authentication, authorization, and admission control.\n\n#### **B. etcd**\n- **Role**: A **distributed key-value store** that stores the cluster’s state and configuration.\n- **Responsibilities**:\n  - Stores all cluster data (e.g., pod specs, service endpoints, secrets).\n  - Acts as the **source of truth** for the cluster.\n  - Supports high availability (HA) with leader election.\n\n#### **C. Scheduler (`kube-scheduler`)**\n- **Role**: Assigns **Pods** to worker nodes.\n- **Responsibilities**:\n  - Watches for newly created Pods with no assigned node.\n  - Selects the best node based on resource availability, constraints, and policies.\n  - Considers factors like CPU/memory requests, taints/tolerations, and affinity/anti-affinity rules.\n\n#### **D. Controller Manager (`kube-controller-manager`)**\n- **Role**: Runs **controller processes** that regulate the cluster’s state.\n- **Responsibilities**:\n  - Ensures the desired state matches the actual state (e.g., if a Pod crashes, it creates a new one).\n  - Includes built-in controllers like:\n    - **Node Controller**: Monitors node health.\n    - **Replication Controller**: Maintains the correct number of Pods.\n    - **Endpoint Controller**: Populates Endpoints objects (links Services to Pods).\n    - **Service Account & Token Controllers**: Manages service accounts and API access tokens.\n\n#### **E. Cloud Controller Manager (Optional)**\n- **Role**:",
